      "editorData": "<h3 style=\"color:#e8eaed;font-size:15px;margin:0 0 8px\">How to export this cluster to MTAT</h3>\n\n<p style=\"color:#9aa4b0;margin:0 0 9px;line-height:1.5\">MTAT (the Broadcom Memory Tiering Assessment Tool) sizes new hosts and prices DRAM\nagainst NVMe, but it reads vCenter, where 30 days of history is kept as 2-hour averages. This\nscreen hands it Operations data instead: true 5-minute peaks. The two tables above are MTAT's\nown import files, column for column.</p>\n\n<p style=\"color:#9aa4b0;margin:0 0 9px;line-height:1.5\"><b style=\"color:#e8eaed\">1. Set the time range</b> at the top of this dashboard to a full business cycle\n&mdash; 30 days at least, longer if month-end or quarter-end matters. Every peak is the Maximum\nover that range. &middot;\n<b style=\"color:#e8eaed\">2. Click a cluster</b> in the first table. &middot;\n<b style=\"color:#e8eaed\">3. Export each table</b> with the widget's CSV export: the hosts table is MTAT's\n<i>Hosts CSV</i>, the VMs table its <i>VMs CSV</i>. Do not open them in Excel first; a re-save\ncan reformat the numbers. &middot;\n<b style=\"color:#e8eaed\">4. In MTAT</b>: connect to vCenter (MTAT requires it even to import), pick the\n<i>same</i> cluster, <b style=\"color:#e8eaed\">Import Data Instead</b> &rarr; <b style=\"color:#e8eaed\">MTAT Export (Hosts + VMs\nCSV)</b>, attach both files, and give the range a label such as\n<span style=\"background:#2a2f36;color:#c8d0d8;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px\">Ops 30d peak</span>. A yellow &ldquo;imported with notes&rdquo; message is normal.</p>\n\n<p style=\"color:#9aa4b0;margin:0 0 9px;line-height:1.5\"><b style=\"color:#e8b84b\">Stop if Active_GB is blank in the hosts table.</b> It is a super metric, and\nsuper metrics arrive disabled: enable the <span style=\"background:#2a2f36;color:#c8d0d8;padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px\">MemTier</span> super metrics in the active\npolicy (Configure &rarr; Policies &rarr; Metrics and Properties) and wait a collection cycle.\nMTAT reads a blank as zero, and a cluster with zero active memory passes every check it has.</p>\n\n<p style=\"color:#9aa4b0;margin:0 0 9px;line-height:1.5\"><b style=\"color:#e8eaed\">Check the import.</b> MTAT's Active Memory total should equal\n<b style=\"color:#e8eaed\">Active peak GB</b> in the first table. Every host shows the same Active_GB on purpose:\nit is the cluster's simultaneous peak shared evenly, because MTAT adds the column up and a sum\nof each host's own peak counts moments that never happened together. Use the\n<b style=\"color:#e8eaed\">Clusters</b> dashboard for real per-host figures.</p>\n\n<p style=\"color:#9aa4b0;margin:0 0 9px;line-height:1.5\"><b style=\"color:#e8eaed\">What MTAT will not know.</b> <b style=\"color:#e8eaed\">Tiering</b> shows <i>Unknown</i> and\n<b style=\"color:#e8eaed\">Pinned_Memory</b> <i>N/A</i>; read pinned memory from the Clusters dashboard, and treat\nit as DRAM in any design. Consumed_GB is the peak, where MTAT's own collection uses the average.\nMTAT reserves no DRAM for vSAN, NSX or the VMkernel &mdash; at 1:1 that rarely changes the\nanswer, at higher ratios it can.</p>",
